<article class="env-lemma" id="0BK7">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BK7">Lemma <span data-tag="0BK7">13.34.3</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{A}$ be an abelian category with countable products and enough injectives. Let $(K_ n)$ be an inverse system of $D^+(\mathcal{A})$. Then $R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits K_ n$ exists. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It suffices to show that $\prod K_ n$ exists in $D(\mathcal{A})$. For every $n$ we can represent $K_ n$ by a bounded below complex $I_ n^\bullet $ of injectives (Lemma <a href="/tag/013K" data-tag="013K">13.18.3</a>). Then $\prod K_ n$ is represented by $\prod I_ n^\bullet $, see Lemma <a href="/tag/0BK6" data-tag="0BK6">13.31.5</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>